fund-java/05-interfaces-and-inheritance/.explanation:
The subclasses implement only the hooks; the base class owns the algorithm:

```java
static final class Savings extends Account {
    Savings(long balance) { super(balance); }
    @Override protected long fee(long cents) { return Math.max(50, cents / 100); }
}
static final class Checking extends Account {
    Checking(long balance) { super(balance); }
    @Override protected long fee(long cents) { return 0; }
    @Override protected long overdraftLimit() { return 10_000; }
}
```

Because `withdraw` is `final`, a subclass cannot "fix" one case by rewriting the whole method, and the tests call it through an `Account` reference, so they exercise real dispatch. The fee needs both parts: `cents / 100` instead of `Math.max(50, cents / 100)` fails "savings pays the minimum fee". Forgetting the `overdraftLimit` override in `Checking` leaves the default 0, and "checking may overdraw up to 100.00 for free" fails. The limit is inclusive: exactly minus 10000 is allowed, one cent past it is refused, and the balance must stay unchanged on a refusal, which is why the base class checks before it subtracts.

